[[:db/retract 17592186197966 :answer-field/choices 17592186197970] [:db/retract 17592186197966 :answer-field/choices 17592186197971] [:db/retract 17592186197966 :answer-field/choices 17592186197967] {:db/id 17592186198405 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a6e0abbd-abc7-5917-a015-ff462bc321f2" "question-74a32659-ab2b-5195-9c2f-351117b323e4" "question-4c584a36-c1bc-53fc-9ad2-f5f8790f1c21"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198047 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= 2x^2 + 3y^2  = 1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,6y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=4\\lambda x \\\\ 1=6\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda )=0 \\\\ \\lambda y=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  = 1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)^{2}+3{y}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3} \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda = \\dfrac12$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}y = \\dfrac{1}{6} \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad y = \\dfrac1{3}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  =1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+3{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3} \\right), \\qquad  \\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $\\left(0, \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$ | $\\left(0, -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$ | $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3}$ | $-\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right),$ and its maximum value is $\\dfrac23.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198366 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7c5088ac-9a02-5898-afc8-f9931b4dcdf9" "question-844fd53e-ecbf-5f52-be0b-e7df67880b9a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198240 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨6x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=\\left(0,  0 \\right).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= 3x^2 + 2y^2  = 16.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=16\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨6x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨6x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}6x=6\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(2\\lambda +1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  =16$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2} & =16\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  =16$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & =16\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{4\\sqrt 3}{3}, 0 \\right).$\n\n- Note that assuming $\\lambda = 1$ from the first equation leads to $y=0,$ which we already have a solution for, and $\\lambda = -\\dfrac12$ from the second equation leads to $x=0,$ which we also have a solution for. So, we have found all the candidates on the boundary.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left( 0,0 \\right), \\qquad  \\left( 0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right), \\qquad \\left(\\pm \\dfrac{4\\sqrt 3}{3}, 0\\right).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $\\left( 0,0 \\right)$ | $\\left( 0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right)$ | $\\left( \\pm \\dfrac{4\\sqrt 3}{3}, 0 \\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-8$ | $16$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $\\left(0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2  \\right),$ and its minimum value is $-8.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198367 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e9c4a6df-1087-507e-b451-15931ae49f9b" "question-1eb20554-7ec2-5620-86b4-093c81339236" "question-71aa525f-c293-54c2-9046-61ecb78eb1e5" "question-302ed073-7778-5ce1-8530-7759debd7de4" "question-7a65655b-2417-5001-b909-d38270394c2a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198241 :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = \\cos{x}+\\cos{y}+\\cos{z},\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\pi.\n$$\n\nSince the angles are acute, we also have the constraints $0 \\lt x,y,z \\lt \\dfrac\\pi 2.$ \n\nLet $g(x,y,z) = x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y,z), \\qquad g(x,y,z) = \\pi\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\lambda  \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\lambda  \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡z=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sine function is one-to-one on the interval $\\left( 0, \\dfrac \\pi 2\\right),$ we can conclude that $x = y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y =x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =\\pi  \\\\ x+x+x & =\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's easy to show that this point is indeed the maximum and not the minimum. To do that, we can compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & ≈1.47 \\\\  & <f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-a6e0abbd-abc7-5917-a015-ff462bc321f2" :question/id #uuid "a6e0abbd-abc7-5917-a015-ff462bc321f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-162781" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4y$ given that $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=4\\lambda x \\\\ -4=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda )=0 \\\\ \\lambda y=-2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}y=-2\\,⇒\\,y=-4.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+(-4)^{2}=4\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich has no solutions.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(0,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-8$ | $8$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,2),$ and its minimum value is $-8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1ab2ac1e-1760-5933-be14-b547164eb087"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1ab2ac1e-1760-5933-be14-b547164eb087" :answer-field/id #uuid "1ab2ac1e-1760-5933-be14-b547164eb087" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b2fc2520-2872-5f53-b5da-91567e75c405" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bc3270e4-ba18-50fc-871d-abe0c18290ef" "answer-e475e7cd-dfed-5fbd-ae10-5b0c7895d481" "answer-b2fc2520-2872-5f53-b5da-91567e75c405" "answer-29ba9e62-696e-5df4-a785-8720b21f1d7e" "answer-26df7659-281a-55ad-9717-8a068ed8d410"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc3270e4-ba18-50fc-871d-abe0c18290ef" :answer/id #uuid "bc3270e4-ba18-50fc-871d-abe0c18290ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e475e7cd-dfed-5fbd-ae10-5b0c7895d481" :answer/id #uuid "e475e7cd-dfed-5fbd-ae10-5b0c7895d481" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2fc2520-2872-5f53-b5da-91567e75c405" :answer/id #uuid "b2fc2520-2872-5f53-b5da-91567e75c405" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29ba9e62-696e-5df4-a785-8720b21f1d7e" :answer/id #uuid "29ba9e62-696e-5df4-a785-8720b21f1d7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26df7659-281a-55ad-9717-8a068ed8d410" :answer/id #uuid "26df7659-281a-55ad-9717-8a068ed8d410" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-74a32659-ab2b-5195-9c2f-351117b323e4" :question/id #uuid "74a32659-ab2b-5195-9c2f-351117b323e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162778" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y$ given that ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ \\lambda y=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system gives \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{2}).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})$ | $(0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})$ | $(\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{2}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{3}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-414dce70-34d5-521d-80c0-bd1e958ec286"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-414dce70-34d5-521d-80c0-bd1e958ec286" :answer-field/id #uuid "414dce70-34d5-521d-80c0-bd1e958ec286" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d21bbc09-d6e4-59ee-99da-258932620c87" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c3a79e2b-437a-5e9b-ac4c-f92bd6d086fb" "answer-c11354ec-35c7-5aa0-87c4-54a1c16aac24" "answer-d21bbc09-d6e4-59ee-99da-258932620c87" "answer-516e7492-e083-54f4-b969-813cd1e7e379" "answer-b9b0972e-d4be-5359-ad60-bb6bb0f92728"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3a79e2b-437a-5e9b-ac4c-f92bd6d086fb" :answer/id #uuid "c3a79e2b-437a-5e9b-ac4c-f92bd6d086fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c11354ec-35c7-5aa0-87c4-54a1c16aac24" :answer/id #uuid "c11354ec-35c7-5aa0-87c4-54a1c16aac24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d21bbc09-d6e4-59ee-99da-258932620c87" :answer/id #uuid "d21bbc09-d6e4-59ee-99da-258932620c87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-516e7492-e083-54f4-b969-813cd1e7e379" :answer/id #uuid "516e7492-e083-54f4-b969-813cd1e7e379" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9b0972e-d4be-5359-ad60-bb6bb0f92728" :answer/id #uuid "b9b0972e-d4be-5359-ad60-bb6bb0f92728" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198946 :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-2y$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨x,-2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=9\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨x,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda )=0 \\\\ \\lambda y=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=9$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 3).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}y=-1\\,⇒\\,y=-2.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=9$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(-2)^{2}=9\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\sqrt{5},-2).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 3),\\,(\\pm \\sqrt{5},-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(0,-3)$ | $(\\pm \\sqrt{5},-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-6$ | $6$ | $\\frac{13}{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,3),$ and its minimum value is $-6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186197546 :answer-field/correct 17592186197548 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199016 :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=10+x+y$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical points in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1=2\\lambda x \\\\ 1=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\ny=x=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ | $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $10+\\sqrt{2}$ | $10-\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}),$ and its minimum value is $10-\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186197966 :answer-field/correct "answer-ada4dc81-6317-551f-a35f-ec559d59d817" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7b766f86-afe6-5a6b-af92-1c826d2db774" "answer-ada4dc81-6317-551f-a35f-ec559d59d817" "answer-9cff40e6-e6af-52b0-9794-92138bab247c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b766f86-afe6-5a6b-af92-1c826d2db774" :answer/id #uuid "7b766f86-afe6-5a6b-af92-1c826d2db774" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10+\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ada4dc81-6317-551f-a35f-ec559d59d817" :answer/id #uuid "ada4dc81-6317-551f-a35f-ec559d59d817" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10-\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9cff40e6-e6af-52b0-9794-92138bab247c" :answer/id #uuid "9cff40e6-e6af-52b0-9794-92138bab247c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10-\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4c584a36-c1bc-53fc-9ad2-f5f8790f1c21" :question/id #uuid "4c584a36-c1bc-53fc-9ad2-f5f8790f1c21" :question/math-academy-id "q-99270" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2x-3y$ given that ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\\le 14.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-3⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=14.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=14\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-3⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,6y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2=2\\lambda x \\\\ -3=6\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,x=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Substituting $x=-2y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=14,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =14 \\\\ 4{y}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =14 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-2y=∓2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}),\\,(2\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ | $(2\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-7\\sqrt{2}$ | $7\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-2\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}),$ and its minimum value is $-7\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6424ee71-260c-5a9c-9520-211ab5878bce"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6424ee71-260c-5a9c-9520-211ab5878bce" :answer-field/id #uuid "6424ee71-260c-5a9c-9520-211ab5878bce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-75897010-0df0-514d-90ad-2e160e86599e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-69bcd4e4-7735-5f84-ad2b-2296929423e2" "answer-df3ed43d-6192-5e7c-985a-432c70a7ae8e" "answer-75897010-0df0-514d-90ad-2e160e86599e" "answer-346a190f-bdeb-55f3-9684-8f7a67706bc3" "answer-ae3016dc-494e-58a7-af10-066972c0ac56"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69bcd4e4-7735-5f84-ad2b-2296929423e2" :answer/id #uuid "69bcd4e4-7735-5f84-ad2b-2296929423e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df3ed43d-6192-5e7c-985a-432c70a7ae8e" :answer/id #uuid "df3ed43d-6192-5e7c-985a-432c70a7ae8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75897010-0df0-514d-90ad-2e160e86599e" :answer/id #uuid "75897010-0df0-514d-90ad-2e160e86599e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-346a190f-bdeb-55f3-9684-8f7a67706bc3" :answer/id #uuid "346a190f-bdeb-55f3-9684-8f7a67706bc3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae3016dc-494e-58a7-af10-066972c0ac56" :answer/id #uuid "ae3016dc-494e-58a7-af10-066972c0ac56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7c5088ac-9a02-5898-afc8-f9931b4dcdf9" :question/id #uuid "7c5088ac-9a02-5898-afc8-f9931b4dcdf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162800" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$ given that ${x}^{2}+y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+y=2.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=2\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-4y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives us the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -4y=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ y=-\\frac{\\lambda }{4}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=2$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation gives $y=-\\frac{1}{4},$ and the constraint condition $g(x,y)=2$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4} & =2\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-8$ | $\\frac{17}{8}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\pm \\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{17}{8}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9450a1b5-7499-5e9b-839d-b2e01f9b40ad"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9450a1b5-7499-5e9b-839d-b2e01f9b40ad" :answer-field/id #uuid "9450a1b5-7499-5e9b-839d-b2e01f9b40ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-980a9376-7517-58ca-ab4f-a3dde8b299a5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0bacdbdc-a887-5be8-858f-c82cd078c3e2" "answer-5d8fe052-2121-5c2b-b562-5b0acfc936f7" "answer-42ad9543-9de3-59fd-b83c-d669cb4d5f6e" "answer-980a9376-7517-58ca-ab4f-a3dde8b299a5" "answer-8edd449c-a525-5941-972e-625e39f9c583"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0bacdbdc-a887-5be8-858f-c82cd078c3e2" :answer/id #uuid "0bacdbdc-a887-5be8-858f-c82cd078c3e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d8fe052-2121-5c2b-b562-5b0acfc936f7" :answer/id #uuid "5d8fe052-2121-5c2b-b562-5b0acfc936f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42ad9543-9de3-59fd-b83c-d669cb4d5f6e" :answer/id #uuid "42ad9543-9de3-59fd-b83c-d669cb4d5f6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-980a9376-7517-58ca-ab4f-a3dde8b299a5" :answer/id #uuid "980a9376-7517-58ca-ab4f-a3dde8b299a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{17}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8edd449c-a525-5941-972e-625e39f9c583" :answer/id #uuid "8edd449c-a525-5941-972e-625e39f9c583" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{19}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199017 :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-4y$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 18.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x-4,2y-4⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(2,2).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=18.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=18\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x-4,2y-4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =1$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 1,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-4=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y-4=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{2}{1-\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{1-\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,x=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x$ in the condition $g(x,y)=18,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(3,3),\\,(-3,-3),\\,(2,2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(3,3)$ | $(-3,-3)$ | $(2,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-6$ | $42$ | $-8$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(-3,-3),$ and its maximum value is $42.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186197972 :answer-field/correct 17592186197973 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-844fd53e-ecbf-5f52-be0b-e7df67880b9a" :question/id #uuid "844fd53e-ecbf-5f52-be0b-e7df67880b9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-99269" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1$ on the region ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ \n\n*Hint*: The critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1$ is $(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNow, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(1+\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 1).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 1,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 1),\\,(\\pm 1,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 1)$ | $(\\pm 1,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $0$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(1,0)$ and $(-1,0),$ and its maximum value is $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-19aab73f-64a8-503f-bc48-afdb40d1b349"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-19aab73f-64a8-503f-bc48-afdb40d1b349" :answer-field/id #uuid "19aab73f-64a8-503f-bc48-afdb40d1b349" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7e355633-a4fd-5563-8a10-1942f94216cb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3c29a590-dcf0-522c-a623-683c02104183" "answer-8dcf8010-52ab-596f-ad39-55c691d8ce16" "answer-7b41dfb4-e096-5076-85d5-9d5b00760c44" "answer-7e355633-a4fd-5563-8a10-1942f94216cb" "answer-68f0e7aa-df4e-574a-b6b5-72d347bb99db"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c29a590-dcf0-522c-a623-683c02104183" :answer/id #uuid "3c29a590-dcf0-522c-a623-683c02104183" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8dcf8010-52ab-596f-ad39-55c691d8ce16" :answer/id #uuid "8dcf8010-52ab-596f-ad39-55c691d8ce16" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b41dfb4-e096-5076-85d5-9d5b00760c44" :answer/id #uuid "7b41dfb4-e096-5076-85d5-9d5b00760c44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e355633-a4fd-5563-8a10-1942f94216cb" :answer/id #uuid "7e355633-a4fd-5563-8a10-1942f94216cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68f0e7aa-df4e-574a-b6b5-72d347bb99db" :answer/id #uuid "68f0e7aa-df4e-574a-b6b5-72d347bb99db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199022 :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}$ given that $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16.$ \n\n*Hint: The critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<16$ is $(0,0).$*" :question/worked-solution "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=16\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,-6y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4x=8\\lambda x \\\\ -6y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(3+\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=16$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =16\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 4).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=16$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2} & =16\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 4),\\,(\\pm 2,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 4)$ | $(\\pm 2,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $-48$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,\\pm 4),$ and its minimum value is $-48.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198002 :answer-field/correct 17592186198006 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281583 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}=5.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨\\frac{x}{2},-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-x=\\frac{\\lambda x}{2} \\\\ 2y=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda +2)=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- If $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, then assuming the constraint condition $g(x,y)=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =5\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\sqrt{20},0).$\n\n- If $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, then from the first equation we have $x=0$, and assuming the constraint condition $g(x,y)=5$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2} & =5\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not possible.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(\\pm \\sqrt{20},0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(\\pm \\sqrt{20},0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-6$ | $-16$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,0),$ and its maximum value is $-6.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-e9c4a6df-1087-507e-b451-15931ae49f9b" :question/id #uuid "e9c4a6df-1087-507e-b451-15931ae49f9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-99212" :question/problem "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the maximum product of three positive numbers whose sum is $6.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the three numbers. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xyz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nx+y+z=6.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=6\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}yz=\\lambda  \\\\ xz=\\lambda  \\\\ xy=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}yz & =xz \\\\ yz-xz & =0 \\\\ (y-x)z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z>0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y=x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =6 \\\\ x+x+x & =6 \\\\ 3x & =6 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(2,2,2),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\nf(2,2,2)=2(2)(2)=8.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2,3) & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & <f(2,2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1cf33480-9f37-5182-be13-a8b96cd44b0f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1cf33480-9f37-5182-be13-a8b96cd44b0f" :answer-field/id #uuid "1cf33480-9f37-5182-be13-a8b96cd44b0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b2ae2756-802d-5b41-9dca-7de890bbf62f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-df22211c-bfff-5df5-835e-9c7677bd0d69" "answer-a5060ca5-f543-5a69-b40d-b6cb40995df0" "answer-65f7137a-1fe6-5c10-bc6a-aaf8ef882fd5" "answer-468904ab-8ce0-59dc-b991-04c016bfdd60" "answer-b2ae2756-802d-5b41-9dca-7de890bbf62f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df22211c-bfff-5df5-835e-9c7677bd0d69" :answer/id #uuid "df22211c-bfff-5df5-835e-9c7677bd0d69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5060ca5-f543-5a69-b40d-b6cb40995df0" :answer/id #uuid "a5060ca5-f543-5a69-b40d-b6cb40995df0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65f7137a-1fe6-5c10-bc6a-aaf8ef882fd5" :answer/id #uuid "65f7137a-1fe6-5c10-bc6a-aaf8ef882fd5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-468904ab-8ce0-59dc-b991-04c016bfdd60" :answer/id #uuid "468904ab-8ce0-59dc-b991-04c016bfdd60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2ae2756-802d-5b41-9dca-7de890bbf62f" :answer/id #uuid "b2ae2756-802d-5b41-9dca-7de890bbf62f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1eb20554-7ec2-5620-86b4-093c81339236" :question/id #uuid "1eb20554-7ec2-5620-86b4-093c81339236" :question/math-academy-id "q-99235" :question/problem "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the maximum sum of the square roots of three positive numbers given that the sum of their squares is $27.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be three positive numbers. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}+\\sqrt{z},\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=27.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=27\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}},\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}=2\\lambda x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}=2\\lambda y \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-3/2}=4\\lambda  \\\\ {y}^{-3/2}=4\\lambda  \\\\ {z}^{-3/2}=4\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$\n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =27 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =27 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =27 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(3,3,3),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\nf(3,3,3)=\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}=3\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5,1,1) & =\\sqrt{5}+1+1 \\\\  & =2+\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & <f(3,3,3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3dba08d8-907e-5202-bcd7-a010c639be80"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3dba08d8-907e-5202-bcd7-a010c639be80" :answer-field/id #uuid "3dba08d8-907e-5202-bcd7-a010c639be80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2994103a-4373-5061-96dc-5e3036bdaad6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8f6c4308-b2fa-5fef-b8c1-c5199b6fd2aa" "answer-2994103a-4373-5061-96dc-5e3036bdaad6" "answer-a4b6bde5-927c-502e-9679-dbf365afa213" "answer-b8f2b83f-9511-5453-b261-063e160979bb" "answer-bab5edc3-664a-5cc1-98e9-141f93a0f45d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f6c4308-b2fa-5fef-b8c1-c5199b6fd2aa" :answer/id #uuid "8f6c4308-b2fa-5fef-b8c1-c5199b6fd2aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2994103a-4373-5061-96dc-5e3036bdaad6" :answer/id #uuid "2994103a-4373-5061-96dc-5e3036bdaad6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4b6bde5-927c-502e-9679-dbf365afa213" :answer/id #uuid "a4b6bde5-927c-502e-9679-dbf365afa213" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8f2b83f-9511-5453-b261-063e160979bb" :answer/id #uuid "b8f2b83f-9511-5453-b261-063e160979bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bab5edc3-664a-5cc1-98e9-141f93a0f45d" :answer/id #uuid "bab5edc3-664a-5cc1-98e9-141f93a0f45d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-71aa525f-c293-54c2-9046-61ecb78eb1e5" :question/id #uuid "71aa525f-c293-54c2-9046-61ecb78eb1e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-162796" :question/problem "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, find the maximum value of the sum of the cosines of three non-negative angles whose sum is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nWe also have the constraints $0\\le x,y,z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\lambda  \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\lambda  \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡z=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sine function is one-to-one on the interval $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y=x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ x+x+x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 3x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0,0) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & <f(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d16c3309-85b6-5b20-bebc-f505a6812eb7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d16c3309-85b6-5b20-bebc-f505a6812eb7" :answer-field/id #uuid "d16c3309-85b6-5b20-bebc-f505a6812eb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4026557b-c261-56b2-bc95-94093b494f38" :answer-field/choices ["answer-502949fc-7dbe-5b07-85d8-6afa9e12632d" "answer-79fcac3f-eebf-518e-b439-901998e02ca4" "answer-2c1a0328-21a2-5d4a-be72-a0e945edf28f" "answer-4026557b-c261-56b2-bc95-94093b494f38" "answer-ecd42a19-38cc-5465-a5d7-71dd32273277"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-502949fc-7dbe-5b07-85d8-6afa9e12632d" :answer/id #uuid "502949fc-7dbe-5b07-85d8-6afa9e12632d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79fcac3f-eebf-518e-b439-901998e02ca4" :answer/id #uuid "79fcac3f-eebf-518e-b439-901998e02ca4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c1a0328-21a2-5d4a-be72-a0e945edf28f" :answer/id #uuid "2c1a0328-21a2-5d4a-be72-a0e945edf28f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4026557b-c261-56b2-bc95-94093b494f38" :answer/id #uuid "4026557b-c261-56b2-bc95-94093b494f38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ecd42a19-38cc-5465-a5d7-71dd32273277" :answer/id #uuid "ecd42a19-38cc-5465-a5d7-71dd32273277" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-302ed073-7778-5ce1-8530-7759debd7de4" :question/id #uuid "302ed073-7778-5ce1-8530-7759debd7de4" :question/math-academy-id "q-99203" :question/problem "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the maximum value of the sum of the sines of angle measures of a triangle." :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles of the triangle. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $0<x,y,z<\\pi .$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=\\pi \n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\operatorname{cos}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\lambda  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡y=\\lambda  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡z=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the cosine function is one-to-one on the interval $(0,\\pi ),$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y=x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =\\pi  \\\\ x+x+x & =\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1+\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & <f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6cb2db16-47e5-539e-a0cd-d00a57818d78"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6cb2db16-47e5-539e-a0cd-d00a57818d78" :answer-field/id #uuid "6cb2db16-47e5-539e-a0cd-d00a57818d78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fe28be55-c646-5b66-9d92-47cfae8c06d1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5ea94620-3d10-5b50-b37f-bb7f9b5dfd37" "answer-cdf3e77a-01de-506d-9a3b-29ed6a80b5d7" "answer-11ccbc00-9325-556b-a2d6-4c11af9d456e" "answer-fe28be55-c646-5b66-9d92-47cfae8c06d1" "answer-633619bf-d174-511b-822e-3e895a240fe7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ea94620-3d10-5b50-b37f-bb7f9b5dfd37" :answer/id #uuid "5ea94620-3d10-5b50-b37f-bb7f9b5dfd37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1+\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cdf3e77a-01de-506d-9a3b-29ed6a80b5d7" :answer/id #uuid "cdf3e77a-01de-506d-9a3b-29ed6a80b5d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2+\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11ccbc00-9325-556b-a2d6-4c11af9d456e" :answer/id #uuid "11ccbc00-9325-556b-a2d6-4c11af9d456e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3+\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe28be55-c646-5b66-9d92-47cfae8c06d1" :answer/id #uuid "fe28be55-c646-5b66-9d92-47cfae8c06d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-633619bf-d174-511b-822e-3e895a240fe7" :answer/id #uuid "633619bf-d174-511b-822e-3e895a240fe7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7a65655b-2417-5001-b909-d38270394c2a" :question/id #uuid "7a65655b-2417-5001-b909-d38270394c2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162794" :question/problem "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, find the minimum value of the sum of the cosines of three obtuse angles whose sum is $2\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nSince the angles are obtuse, we also have the constraints $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x,y,z<\\pi .$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=2\\pi \n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\lambda  \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\lambda  \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡z=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sine function is one-to-one on the interval $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ),$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y=x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =2\\pi  \\\\ x+x+x & =2\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =2\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}),$ and the minimum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & >f(\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8d8f710c-79f7-5edc-ac43-a4415b3fc381"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8d8f710c-79f7-5edc-ac43-a4415b3fc381" :answer-field/id #uuid "8d8f710c-79f7-5edc-ac43-a4415b3fc381" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d06bf348-df0d-5a6d-aad0-f9e69842ba38" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d45d1e89-aa02-5b80-b287-947a5fb81ff6" "answer-808a35e9-d9c9-5168-90b3-b4d092b5569b" "answer-f8d50612-5767-50aa-8cb3-8d164d717fd6" "answer-75bab5bd-632a-58ef-9eb9-018984699a27" "answer-d06bf348-df0d-5a6d-aad0-f9e69842ba38"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d45d1e89-aa02-5b80-b287-947a5fb81ff6" :answer/id #uuid "d45d1e89-aa02-5b80-b287-947a5fb81ff6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-808a35e9-d9c9-5168-90b3-b4d092b5569b" :answer/id #uuid "808a35e9-d9c9-5168-90b3-b4d092b5569b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8d50612-5767-50aa-8cb3-8d164d717fd6" :answer/id #uuid "f8d50612-5767-50aa-8cb3-8d164d717fd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75bab5bd-632a-58ef-9eb9-018984699a27" :answer/id #uuid "75bab5bd-632a-58ef-9eb9-018984699a27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d06bf348-df0d-5a6d-aad0-f9e69842ba38" :answer/id #uuid "d06bf348-df0d-5a6d-aad0-f9e69842ba38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199484 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the minimum and maximum values of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2+4y^2\n$$\n\nsubject to constraint $g(x,y) = x^2+y^2 \\leq 1.$ In other words, we wish to find the global extrema of $f(x,y)$ defined on the disc of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5ac34145b3911b19eb3775a915e8f0462c78502be6d6292e7682265c86eafce7.png)\n\n  \n\nTo find the extrema, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we find the critical points of $f$ inside the domain $x^2+y^2 < 1.$\n\n- Then, we find the extreme values of $f$ subject to the constraint $x^2+y^2=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers.\n\n- Finally, we determine the maximum and minimum values by evaluating $f$ at the candidate points.\n\nRecall that the critical points of a function $f(x,y)$ are the solutions to $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf 0^T.$ Now, since the derivative of $f$ is the transpose of its gradient $\\nabla f,$ the critical points are also given by the solutions to $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0.$\n\nSo, we can find the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ Note that this point belongs to our domain.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 + y^2  = 1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 8y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(4-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm 1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4-2)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $\\left(\\pm 1, 0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 1, 0), \\qquad (0, \\pm1), \\qquad (0,0).\n$$\n\nNote that we add the critical point $(0,0)$ to the list of the candidates above.\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at these points: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\pm 1,0)$ | $(0, \\pm 1)$ | $(0, 0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum of $0$ at the point $(0, 0)$ and a maximum of $4$ at the points $(0, \\pm 1).$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198494 :activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
